<handout>
<title>Solving Quadratic Equations by Square Roots</title>
<page>
<objectives>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            In this section we will see how to:
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                Solve quadratic equations using square roots.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Recognize when we can use the square root principle.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</objectives>
<p>
    The <term>square root</term> of a number is the inverse of squaring a number. For example if <m>5^2 = 25</m>, then <m>\sqrt{25} = 5</m>. If the square root of a number is 
    another whole number, then the number inside the square root is called <term>a perfect square</term>. If a number is not a prefect square, we can use a calculator to find
    the decimal approximation of the square root or just leave it written inside a square root, such as <m>\sqrt{2}</m>.
</p>
<p>
    Note that <m>(-5)^2 = 25</m>, so <m>\sqrt{25}</m> could also equal <m>-5</m>. So mathematically, <m>\sqrt{25} = \pm 5</m>. In most applications, 
    we are only interested in the positive value, and this is called the <term>principal square root</term>. When we solve a quadratic equation, however, we want both the
    positive and negative values of the number.
</p>
<example workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
        <p>
            <m>x^2=25</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            <m>x=\pm 5</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Take the square root of both sides of the equation. For the number side of the equation, include a <m>\pm</m>. <m>\sqrt{x^2} = \sqrt{25} \rightarrow x = \pm 5</m>.
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>
<example workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
        <p>
            <m>x^2=58</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            <m>x=\pm \sqrt{58}</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Take the square root of both sides of the equation. For the number side of the equation, include a <m>\pm</m>. <m>\sqrt{x^2} = \sqrt{58} \rightarrow x = \pm \sqrt{58}</m>.
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>
</page>
<page>
<p>
    We can use square roots to solve a quadratic equation, if there are only squared terms involving the variable and constants. We may need to perform some preliminary steps to
    isolate the squared variable term first or some steps after taking the square root to isolate the variable.
</p>
<example workspace="1.5in">
       <statement>
        <p>
            <m>6x^2+8=62</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            <m>x=\pm 3</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Start by isolating the squared term. <m>6x^2 + 8 = 62 \rightarrow 6x^2 = 54 \rightarrow x^2 = 9</m>. Take the square root of both sides of the equation. 
            <m>\sqrt{x^2} = \sqrt{9} \rightarrow x = \pm 3</m>.
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>
<example workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
        <p>
            <m>(x-7)^2=25</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            <m>x=12,2</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Start by taking the square root of both sides. Include a <m>\pm</m> on the number side of the equation. <m>\sqrt{(x-7)^2} = \sqrt{25} \rightarrow x-7 = \pm 5</m>.
            Add 7 to both sides and solve the two resulting equations. <m>x = 7 \pm 5 \rightarrow x = 12</m> and <m>x=2</m>.
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>
<example workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
        <p>
            <m>\left(x-\frac{1}{3}\right)^2=\frac{5}{9}</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            <m>x=\frac{1}{3}\pm\frac{\sqrt{5}}{3}</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Start by taking the square root of both sides. Include a <m>\pm</m> on the number side of the equation. <m>\sqrt{\left(x-\frac{1}{3}\right)^2} = \sqrt{\frac{5}{9}}
            \rightarrow x-\frac{1}{3} = \pm \frac{\sqrt{5}}{3}</m>. Add 1/3 to both sides to solve the equations. <m>x=\frac{1}{3}\pm\frac{\sqrt{5}}{3}</m>.
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>
</page>
<page>
<paragraphs>
  <title>Try it Now!</title>  
        <p>
            Solve the following quadratic equations using square roots.
        </p>
    
            <p workspace="1.5in">
           (a) <m>x^2 = 81</m>
            </p>
        
            <p workspace="1.5in">
           (b) <m>x^2 = 65</m>
            </p>
        
            <p workspace="1.5in">
            (c) <m>5x^2 = 125</m>
            </p>
        
            <p workspace="1.5in">
           (d) <m>8x^2+15=407</m>
            </p>
        
            <p workspace="1.5in">
            (e) <m>(x+5)^2=64</m>
            </p>
        
            <p workspace="1.5in">
            (f) <m>(x+4)^2=29</m>
            </p>
        
            <p workspace="1.5in">
            (g) <m>5(x-5)^2+2=22</m>
            </p>
        
    </paragraphs>
    </page>
</handout>